##Will Allred
How’s this for a “buyers journey”
- Scroll X
- See a retweet of someone talking about how they want to start creating AI videos
- Check out video
- Think “I think my friend would find that interesting”
- Page refreshes or something… tweet lost.
- Remembered co name
- Googled to site
- Texted to friend.

Now if you’re tracking the marketing analytics you saw:

Post got a retweet and some good views
Someone found us on Google
Someone else came direct to site
Neither converted

Lesson here. Don’t get too lost in the sauce of being data driven.

Let the data inform what you know to be human behavior. 

You’re doing it right now.

Ps. This post is actually written for anyone still measuring open rate on cold email campaigns 😂

##Josh Braun
Just checking in.

Following up.

Bumping this up.

Wanted to circle back.

If the timing is off, reminding someone you exist doesn’t change the timing.

It just reminds them you’re desperate.

A better approach:

Stay useful.

Instead of asking for an update, send something about that makes them smarter about a topic they’re interested in. 

“This report on churn signals caught my eye. Sharing since you mentioned retention was a focus this year.”

When timing is off, your job isn’t to push the deal forward.

It’s to stay top of mind. 

Because timing changes.
Budgets get approved.
Priorities shift.
Problems get worse.

And when they do, the person who stayed useful is usually remembered before the person who kept checking in.

